== Law Appplicable to Arbitration Proceedings : _Lex Arbitri_

Procedural rules governing arbitration can fall into one of two levels. The 'lex arbitri' is curial law which refers to the *national arbitration law* that sets the framework for arbitration and facilitates the arbitration by providing appointment of arbitrators or overseeing the adherence of the award in accordance with national standards. Then there is procedural rules of non-state origin or institutional rules which apply to the conduct of proceedings found in the institution where the arbitration is to be conducted.

In JKERA v. Rash Builders, Srinagar was designated as the seat of the arbitration tribunal. Accordingly, J&K High Court referred parties to arbitration. The tribunal was situated in Delhi. Final award was signed in Delhi. Since seat was in Srinagar, set-aside under Section 34 came to be filed before Jammu Kashmir High Court. However, the court declined to entertain the petition, holding that since the proceedings were conducted in New Delhi, the Courts of New Delhi alone had jurisdiction.

Holding that the seat court that is Jammu Kashmir High Court would have the correct jurisdiction, the Court laid down basic principles applicable to the concept of seat:
+ Seat of arbitration is the juridical home or the legal place of arbitration. It determines the curial law and identifies courts having supervisory control over that proceeding.

+ Once the seat is designated, the courts of that place alone have exclusive jurisdiction to entertain all proceedings arising out of the arbitration, including challenges to the award. In that sense, it functions akin to an exclusive jurisdiction clause
+ The venue is merely the geographical location chosen for the convenience of holding hearings, examination of witnesses or meetings of the arbitral tribunal.
+ Mere fact that the arbitration proceedings were conducted or rendered at a particular place does not confer jurisdiction to the courts of that place if it is different from the designated seat.
+ Where the seat has not been explicitly designated, courts may determine the seat by applying two tests:
  + Courts could construe venue as seat by the application of *Shashoua Principles.*

  + Or through the closest or most intimate connection test based on the Naviera Amazonica Principle. (Enercon)
+ The intention of parties as discerned from the agreement and its surrounding circumstances is the paramount factor in determining the seat

=== Roger Shashoua v. Mukesh Sharma

==== Facts
- An anti-suit injunction was filed by Roger S. against Mukesh S. which was the principal issue before the Queen's Bench.
- The agreement contemplated between the parties was such that:
  + Clause 17 provided that the agreement was to be governed by the laws of India
  + Clause 14 was the arbitration clause and provided for arbitration to be conducted as per the International Chamber of Commerce Rules
  + It also provided that the *venue of arbitration* shall be London UK.

+ The dispute before the court is what is the seat court having supervisory jurisdiction over the proceedings. Mukesh submits that it is Indian law while Roger submits that it is English law.
+ An interim measure for protection under section 7 was filed before the New Delhi High Court for claiming status quo on the assets. At this time, the claimant submitted that the seat was in London and the arbitration agreement was governed by English law. Yet interim relief was claimed before Indian courts due to the presence of assets of the respondent in India.
+ Simultaneously, a request for arbitration was filed before the courts of England
+ The respondent applied to the Delhi High Court for declaration that there existed no valid arbitration agreement between it and the other parties. The High Court rejected this plea.

==== Discussion of the Court

+ It was unanimously accepted that the capacity to give anti-suit injunction was only with the courts of the seat. It was submitted that in the ordinary course the venue decided by the parties would constitute the seat of the arbitration. If a venue was named and no other juridical seat was specified, it would be expected that the venue would also constitute the seat

+ Conversely, it was submitted that the term venue is not synonymous with seat and in consideration of relevant circumstances the juridical seat must be in India and the curial law must be Indian law.

+ The court held that designation of London as the venue also amounts to designation of juridical seat. The parties have not simply provided the location of hearings to be in London for the sake of convenience. It was conversely submitted that since the proper law of the matrix contract is Indian law, the law governing the agreement to arbitrate must also be Indian law.

+ It did not matter whether the governing law of the matrix contract was Indian law. Since England is the seat of arbitration, it carries with it something akin to "exclusive jurisdiction clause" and thus, the foreign court's shall not be responsible.

=== Preliminary Award Made in Case No. 2321/1974 (ICC)

The arbitration was conducted between a State and a public authority of Sweden. Dispute arose in respect of a commercial contract between the State and the public authority.

The provisions of the contract provided that one or more arbitrators be appointed according to the ICC Rules. However, the contract provided that the appointment of arbitrator shall be undertaken by authority other than the Court of Arbitration of the ICC. The nominated authority refused to appoint the arbitrator and the Court of Arbitration considered that it was up to them to proceed with the nomination of the arbitrator.

It was this decision which the defendants claimed to be invalid. A challenge was made before the Ld. Arbitrator. He upheld the appointment by the Court of Arbitration on the following grounds

+ The fact that an arbitration agreement must be interpreted strictly is not a principle that can be mechanically applied, specifically when it comes to the effectiveness or the validity of the clause.

+ Parties must be presumed to have intended to establish an effective machinery for settlement of disputes.
+ The nominated authority refused to appoint the arbitrator saying that he considered the court of arbitration as the valid avenue for appointment of arbitrator.
+ The rules of ICC have been incorporated by reference. They require that the Court of Arbitration appoint the arbitrator failing agreement by the parties.
+ Since their aggrieved method proved to be ineffective, according to the ICC rules the matter shall then be governed by the Court of Arbitration.

The objective of the Tribunal herein was to ensure that even if there is a conflict between the language of the contract, and rules incorporated by reference, the arbitration agreement itself is made workable. Since an interpretation that would render the clause otiose would be against the presumed intention of the parties to arbitrate.

=== AQZ v. ARA

+ Parties had applied to the Singapore High Court for setting aside a Ruling and Partial Award on Preliminary Issue

+ The dispute concerned whether parties to a supply contract for non-cooking coal had agreed to a second supply contract or whether they had reached merely preliminary oral agreement on certain points. When the seller did not deliver the goods, the buyer initiated proceedings before SIAC in 2013.

+ The 2010 SIAC rules provided that certain disputes should be referred to an expedited procedure before a sole arbitrator. The contract, however, provided for a bench of three arbitrators. The seller participated in the proceedings under protest having reserved its rights to challenge the effectiveness of the arbitration agreement.

+ In its partial award, the sole arbitrator ruled on its jurisdiction and held that the tribunal is competent to determine issues arising out of the contract and that the parties had, in fact, entered into a second contract which was breached by the seller.
+ The seller argued that the composition of the tribunal was not in accordance with the agreement of the parties.
  + The application of Rule 5 of the SIAC Rules 2010 for expedited proceedings was incorrect since the arbitration agreement had concluded at a time when SIAC Rules 2007 were under force which did not provide for expedited proceedings.
  + The appointment of sole arbitrator, despite a provision in the arbitration agreement to the contrary, was a serious procedural flaw.

==== Court's Reasoning

The court rejected both challenges for the following reasons

+ The clause providing for arbitration merely stated that the dispute #quote()[shall be settled by arbitration in accordance with the rules of conciliation and arbitration of the Singapore International Arbitration Centre by three arbitrators in the English language]

+ There is a presumption that reference to rules of a tribunal refers to such rules that are applicable on the date of commencement of arbitration and not on the date of contract, provided that the rules contain mainly procedural provisions. If the rules contain substantive provisions, then those in force at the date of contract would apply.
+ Supplier argued that the contract does not include the phrase for the time being in force. Therefore, the intention was not to apply the SIAC Rules in force at the time of the commencement of arbitration. However, the court held that the absence of this phrase does not displace the presumption in favour of the SIAC Rules in force.

+ Now once the SIAC Rules 2010 have been incorporated into the party's contract by reference the clause of the contract will have to be interpreted purposively.
+ A commercially sensible approach therefore would be to interpret the intention of the parties to arbitrate and to recognize that the SIAC President has the discretion to appoint the sole arbitrator.
+ The ICC's new emergency arbitration provisions provide explicitly that they don't apply to arbitration agreements entered into before the new rules came into force. The SIAC Expedited Procedure provisions do not contain an exclusion of this sort, which fortifies the conclusion that the expedited procedure under SIAC rules could override the party's agreement for arbitration even if the provisions came *after* the contract came into force.

=== Union of India v. McDonell Douglas Corporation.

The question was simply whether the arbitration was governed by the laws of India or by the laws of England.

The agreement in question contained an arbitration clause which was in the following terms

+ The dispute or differences arising out of or in connection with the agreement shall be referred to an arbitration tribunal consisting of three members.
+ The arbitration shall be conducted in accordance with the procedure provided in the Indian Arbitration Act.
+ The seat of the arbitration proceedings shall be London, United Kingdom

The plaintiffs argued that the words arbitration shall be conducted in accordance with the Indian Arbitration Act makes it clear that the parties have chosen Indian law to govern the arbitration proceedings arising under the agreement. Defendants conversely argue that stipulating London as the seat of arbitration means that English law will govern the arbitration proceedings.


==== Court's Judgment

By Article 11, the parties have not only chosen Indian law to govern the rights and obligations arising out of their commercial bargain, but also the rights and obligations arising out of their agreement to arbitrate. Therefore, the governing law of both the commercial matrix contract and the arbitration agreement is Indian law.

The fact that the law of India is the proper law of the arbitration agreement does not entail that the governing law of *the proceedings themselves* shall also be the law of India. The law that governs the validity of the arbitration agreement is not the law that also governs the procedure of arbitration.

If the parties do not make an express choice of procedure or law, the court will have to consider whether there is an implied choice. However, in this place, the fact that the parties have agreed to a place of arbitration is a pointer to the fact that they implicitly have chosen the laws of that place to govern the procedure of arbitration. The word seat means the *legal place of the arbitration proceedings.* By making this choice explicitly, they have chosen English law to govern the procedure of arbitration proceedings

There is a way of reconciling the two phrases. The phrase conducted implies that Indian law shall govern the way in which the parties and the tribunal carry on their proceedings rather than conferring a supervisory jurisdiction to Indian courts.

Therefore the English Law is to *govern the arbitration proceedings* while Indian Law is concerned with the _*internal conduct of arbitration*_ *insofar as it is not inconsistent* with English arbitral procedural law.

=== Disortho S.A.S v. Meril Life Sciences Private Limited
<disortho-s.a.s-v.-meril-life-sciences-private-limited>
#strong[BYC v. BCZ]

The Singapore High Court observed that the principle of severability does not mean that the arbitration agreement is completely insulated or detached from the main contract. It simply means that the arbitration clause remains valid and enforceable even if the main contract is found to be invalid. Therefore, a choice of law made in the main contract is a strong indicator of the law governing the validity of the arbitration agreement in the absence of contrary indica.

#strong[Arif Azim]

- Part 1 of the Act 1996 and the provisions thereunder only apply where the arbitration takes place in India. That means the seat is in India or the law governing arbitration agreement are the laws of India
- The moment a seat is determined, it would be akin to an exclusive jurisdiction clause whereby only jurisdictional courts of the seat will have jurisdiction to regulate the arbital proceedings
- The closest connection test is no longer viable criteria in view of the Sashua principle
- The better criteria for determining seat would be that where the arbitration agreement expressly designates its place of arbitration anchoring the arbitral proceedings without any contrary indica, such place would be the seat of arbitration
- However, it is not to say that the closest connection test has no application whatsoever. Where there is no express or implied designation of place of arbitration, either of venue or of curial law the closest connection test may be more suitable in determining a seat of arbitration.

#strong[Disortho Facts]

In the facts of Disortho clause 16 stipulated that the agreement shall be governed and construed in accordance with the laws of India. Further, all matters arising from the agreement shall be subject to the jurisdiction of courts in Gujarat. Clause 18 outlines conciliation and arbitration process. The arbitration was to be conducted by the ICC in Bogota the award was to be given as per the Colombian law governing the matter.

#strong[Court's Observations]

- Clause 16 states that the agreement shall be governed by the laws of India and the exclusive jurisdiction shall fall within the courts of Gujarat. Therefore, under clause 18, Bogota has been designated as a venue for conciliation. While the courts in Gujarat retain exclusive jurisdiction over the dispute

- The law governing arbitration agreement is Indian law and therefore the scope and interpretation shall be determined in accordance with Indian law. Most prominently the exclusive jurisdiction is granted to Indian courts which are the supervisory courts in this context
- The test developed by Sulamerica would indicate that there is no express choice at the first place. Therefore, there are two choices: either the law of the seat or that of the venue shall be determined as the governing law. There is a strong presumption, therefore, that lex contractors governs the arbitration agreement. Since the lex contractors here is Indian law, in the absence of any contract in India, that is to be considered as the law governing the arbitration agreement. Therefore, Indian law governs the arbitration agreement and the controversy is to be resolved accordingly



#pagebreak()

== Law Governing Substance of Dispute between the Parties - _Lex Contractus_

- Choice of substantive laws more difficult in ICA since the tribunal is not bound to apply the conflict rules or the substantive law of the place of arbitration. Since the parties belong to different jurisdictions there will always be options to select the applicable law.

Article 28 of the Model Law reads thus:

#quote(block: true)[
  (1)The arbitral tribunal shall decide the dispute in accordance with such rules of law as are chosen by the parties as applicable to the substance of the dispute. Any designation of the law or legal system of a given State shall be construed, unless otherwise expressed, as directly referring to the substantive law of that state and not to its conflict of laws rules.

  (2)Failing any designation by the parties, the arbitral tribunal shall apply the law determined by
  the conflict of laws rules which it considers applicable.

  (3)The arbitral tribunal shall decide ex aequo et bono or as amiable compositeur only if the
  parties have expressly authorized it to do so.

  (4)In all cases, the arbitral tribunal shall decide in accordance with the terms of the contract and
  shall take into account the usages of the trade applicable to the transaction.
]

_(Numbers correspond to the paragraphs above)_
+ It allows the parties to select a binding choice of law to govern the substance of the dispute and provide rules of construction when there is conflict of law.
  - The primary issue is whether parties can choose not only body of law but also other parts of legal codes or set of rules that are not in force as such. These may include international conventions or uniform law that is not in force yet
  - The UNCITRAL working group adopted this view. The phrase rules of law was preferred by the working group since it allows the parties to implicitly designate parts of the systems of law applicable
  - The phrase rule of law implies that parties could choose where to allow national law to apply and where to exclude the provisions on a certain topic. For instance, parties could choose Swiss law as the law of the matrix contract while excluding the rules governing judicially ordered set of further the chosen provisions need not be law as such. The Commission notes that it was stated in support of the term rules of law that it would allow the parties to choose rules embodied in a convention or similar legal text elaborated at a national level even if it is not in force.
+ Here there were two key questions. One was whether the tribunal is empowered to choose the applicable law directly or via conflict of rules, and second is whether the tribunal could choose rules of law and not only law for a given national state, as the parties were permitted under para 1
  - The working group chose a narrower approach requiring the tribunal to take recourse to conflict of law rules since the narrower freedom give greater predictability and certainty to ensure that the tribunal gave reasons for its choice of law.
+ This allows the parties to have the tribunal decide amiable compositeur. Paragraph 4 was adopted as a limitation to the powers of the tribunal under paragraph 3
